\lessonlabel{6}
\section{Karnaugh Maps}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item construct a Karnaugh map for a Boolean function with up to four variables;
  \item recognise all admissible groups of 1's that obey the grouping rules;
  \item form the largest essential groups and write the corresponding product terms;
  \item combine the product terms into a minimal sum of products (SOP) expression;
  \item incorporate don't care conditions when simplifying a map;
  \item verify that the minimal expression is equivalent to the original truth table.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Grouping cells}

A \term{group} (also called an \term{implicant}) is a rectangle of 1's on the Karnaugh map whose size is a power of two: 1, 2, 4, 8 ... cells. Groups may wrap around the left right or top bottom edges of the map. Each group must be as large as possible because a larger group eliminates more variables from the product term. Overlapping groups are allowed when they help to reduce the total number of literals.

\begin{example}
Consider the three variable function \(F(A,B,C)=\sum m(1,2,3,5,7)\). Its Karnaugh map is

\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
      & BC=00 & BC=01 & BC=11 & BC=10 \\ \hline
A=0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\
A=1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\
\end{array}
\]

Possible groups:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A four cell group covering columns \(BC=01\) and \(BC=11\) (both rows). This group eliminates \(A\) and \(C\), leaving the literal \(B\).
  \item A two cell group in the top row, columns \(BC=10\) and \(BC=11\). This group eliminates \(C\), leaving the term \(A\bar B\).
  \item A wrap around two cell group formed by the cells at \((A=0,BC=01)\) and \((A=1,BC=01)\). This group eliminates \(B\) and \(C\), leaving the term \(\bar C\).
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\begin{example}
A four variable function \(G(A,B,C,D)=\sum m(0,2,5,7,8,10,13,15)\) has the map

\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
      & CD=00 & CD=01 & CD=11 & CD=10 \\ \hline
AB=00 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\
AB=01 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\
AB=11 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\
AB=10 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\
\end{array}
\]

Groups that can be formed:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Four cell group covering the leftmost column (\(CD=00\)) in rows \(AB=00\) and \(AB=10\) together with the wrap around cells at \(AB=10,CD=00\) and \(AB=00,CD=10\). This yields the term \(\bar C\bar D\).
  \item Four cell group covering the rightmost two columns (\(CD=01\) and \(CD=11\)) in rows \(AB=01\) and \(AB=11\). This yields the term \(B D\).
  \item Two cell wrap around group formed by the cells at \(AB=10,CD=10\) and \(AB=10,CD=00\). This yields the term \(A\bar B\).
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{Minimal sum of products expressions}

For each group, write a product term that contains only the variables whose value is the same for all cells in the group. If a variable changes within the group, it is omitted. Use the complemented form when the variable is 0 in the group. The minimal SOP is the OR of all product terms obtained from the essential groups. Overlapping groups are permitted when they reduce the total number of literals.

\begin{example}
Using the groups from the first example:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The four cell group (columns \(01\) and \(11\)) gives the term \(B\) because \(B=1\) for all cells, while \(A\) and \(C\) vary.
  \item The two cell top row group gives the term \(A\bar B\) because \(A=1\) and \(B=0\) for both cells, while \(C\) varies.
\end{itemize}
The minimal SOP is therefore
\[
F = B + A\bar B .
\]

A quick algebraic check:
\[
B + A\bar B = (B + A)(B + \bar B) = (B + A)\cdot 1 = A + B ,
\]
which matches the original minterm list.
\end{example}

\begin{example}
From the second example the three groups give the terms \(\bar C\bar D\), \(B D\), and \(A\bar B\). The minimal SOP is

\[
G = \bar C\bar D + B D + A\bar B .
\]

Verification by truth table (omitted) shows that the expression is true for exactly the listed minterms.
\end{example}

\subsection{Using don't care conditions}

A \term{don't care condition} is a minterm for which the function output may be either 0 or 1. In a Karnaugh map these cells are marked with an X. They can be treated as either 1 or 0, whichever helps to form larger groups and thus a simpler expression. Don't care cells are never required to appear in the final SOP; they are only used as optional members of groups.

\begin{example}
Simplify the three variable function \(H(A,B,C)\) defined by minterms \(m(1,2,5)\) and don't care terms \(d(0,7)\). The map is

\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
      & BC=00 & BC=01 & BC=11 & BC=10 \\ \hline
A=0 & X & 1 & 0 & 1 \\
A=1 & 0 & 1 & X & 0 \\
\end{array}
\]

We can form a four cell group that includes the don't care at \((A=0,BC=00)\) and the 1's at \((A=0,BC=01)\), \((A=1,BC=01)\) and the don't care at \((A=1,BC=11)\). This group eliminates \(B\) and leaves the term \(\bar C\). The remaining 1 at \((A=0,BC=10)\) can be paired with the don't care at \((A=1,BC=10)\) to form a two cell group that eliminates \(C\) and yields the term \(\bar B A\). The minimal SOP is therefore

\[
H = \bar C + A\bar B .
\]

Because the don't care cells were used only to enlarge groups, the expression is valid for all required minterms while remaining as simple as possible.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Given a Karnaugh map with possible don't care cells, list every admissible group whose size is a power of two, select the largest essential groups (using don't care cells when helpful), write the product term for each selected group, and combine them into a minimal SOP. Confirm the result by checking that the expression covers all required minterms and does not depend on the don't care cells.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Construct the Karnaugh map for \(F(A,B,C,D)=\sum m(0,2,5,7,8,10,13,15)\). Identify all possible groups, choose the largest essential groups, and write the minimal SOP.
  \item For the function \(K(W,X,Y)=\sum m(1,3,4,6)\) with don't care terms \(d(0,7)\), draw the map, use the don't care cells to form the largest groups, and obtain the minimal SOP.
  \item Explain why a wrap around group that spans the leftmost and rightmost columns of a four variable map is valid, using a concrete example from a map you design.
  \item Reduce the expression \(L = \bar A\bar B C + \bar A B C + A\bar B C + A B C\) by constructing a Karnaugh map and writing the minimal SOP.
  \item Given the map below, form all possible groups of size 2 and 4, then derive two different minimal SOP expressions and show that they are equivalent.
\end{enumerate}

