\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Karnaugh map & A grid that places minterms so that adjacent cells differ in only one variable. \\
Group (implicant) & A rectangle of \(2^{n}\) adjacent 1's (or 0's) on the map; used to simplify Boolean expressions. \\
Essential prime implicant & A group that contains a 1 (or 0) not covered by any other group; it must appear in any minimal expression. \\
Sum of products (SOP) & A Boolean expression formed by OR ing AND terms; each term is a product of literals. \\
Don't care condition & A minterm for which the function output may be either 0 or 1; it can be used to enlarge groups but does not appear in the final expression. \\
Wrap around & The property that the leftmost and rightmost columns (or top and bottom rows) are considered adjacent, allowing groups to cross the map border. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{7}
\section{Sequential Circuits}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the function of a \term{latch};
  \item explain how a \term{flip flop} stores a bit of information;
  \item distinguish between level triggered and edge triggered devices;
  \item illustrate the role of a \term{clock signal} in synchronous circuits;
  \item understand the operation of a \term{master slave flip flop};
  \item recognise the purpose of a \term{JK flip flop} and how it differs from a D flip flop;
  \item explain how a group of flip flops forms a \term{register} for multi bit storage.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Latches}

A \term{latch} is a simple memory element that is transparent while an enable signal is active. The most common latch is the SR (Set Reset) latch built from two cross coupled NAND or NOR gates.

When the enable (often called \texttt{EN}) is low, the outputs keep their previous values. When \texttt{EN} is high, the latch follows the inputs: a high \texttt{S} forces the output \texttt{Q} to one, a high \texttt{R} forces \texttt{Q} to zero. If both \texttt{S} and \texttt{R} are low, the latch holds its state. The latch is level triggered, meaning that any change on the inputs while the enable is active is immediately reflected at the output.

\begin{example}
Consider an SR NAND latch with inputs \texttt{S}=0, \texttt{R}=1 and \texttt{EN}=1. Because \texttt{S}=0 forces the upper NAND output to one, the latch sets \texttt{Q}=1. If later \texttt{EN} goes to zero, the latch retains \texttt{Q}=1 even though the inputs change.
\end{example}

\subsection{Flip Flops}

A \term{flip flop} is a clocked latch that changes state only on a specific clock transition. The basic type is the D (Data) flip flop. It has a data input \texttt{D} and a clock input \texttt{CLK}. On the active clock edge, the value of \texttt{D} is transferred to the output \texttt{Q}.

Because the change occurs only at the clock edge, flip flops are used to build synchronous registers, counters, and state machines. The edge triggered nature prevents the output from reacting to spurious changes on the data line between clock edges, providing reliable storage.

\begin{example}
A D flip flop receives \texttt{D}=0 while \texttt{CLK} is low. When \texttt{CLK} rises from zero to one, the flip flop captures the zero and sets \texttt{Q}=0. The output remains zero until the next rising edge, even if \texttt{D} later becomes one.
\end{example}

\subsection{Clock Signals}

A \term{clock signal} is a periodic square wave that coordinates the operation of synchronous circuits. The two important attributes are frequency (how fast it toggles) and duty cycle (the proportion of the period that the signal is high).

All flip flops in a synchronous design share the same clock. This ensures that data moves through the circuit in lock step, preventing race conditions. The clock edge that triggers a flip flop is called the active edge; designers choose rising or falling edges consistently throughout a design.

\begin{example}
In a four bit register, four D flip flops share a common \texttt{CLK}. When \texttt{CLK} rises, each flip flop captures its respective data bit simultaneously, forming a stable four bit word that can be used by downstream logic.
\end{example}

\subsection{Registers}

A \term{register} is a collection of flip flops that stores a multi bit word. Each flip flop holds one bit, and all bits are updated together on the same clock edge. Registers are the basic building blocks of larger memory structures and are used to hold intermediate results in processors.

Because all bits change at the same instant, a register provides a snapshot of data that can be moved as a unit. This property is essential for pipelined architectures where each stage must work on a consistent set of inputs.

\subsection{Master Slave Flip Flops}

A \term{master slave flip flop} combines two simple flip flops in series to achieve edge triggered behaviour using level triggered components. The first stage, the master, is enabled when the clock is high. The second stage, the slave, is enabled when the clock is low. During the high phase the master follows the data input, while the slave holds its previous value. When the clock transitions low, the master's stored value is transferred to the slave, and the output changes only at that moment. This arrangement eliminates the possibility of the output changing while the clock is high, improving timing reliability in complex designs.

\subsection{JK Flip Flops}

A \term{JK flip flop} extends the D flip flop by providing two data inputs, \texttt{J} and \texttt{K}, and a toggle capability. When the clock edge occurs, the following rules apply: if \texttt{J}=0 and \texttt{K}=0 the output holds its previous state; if \texttt{J}=1 and \texttt{K}=0 the output is set to one; if \texttt{J}=0 and \texttt{K}=1 the output is reset to zero; and if both \texttt{J} and \texttt{K}=1 the output toggles. This flexibility makes the JK flip flop useful in counters and state machines where both set and reset actions are needed without separate control signals.

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Identify a circuit that uses a latch and one that uses a flip flop. Explain how the presence or absence of a clock signal changes their behavior.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Draw the truth table of an SR NAND latch and indicate the hold condition.
  \item Explain what happens to a D flip flop if the clock signal is noisy and contains extra edges.
  \item A circuit uses a latch for a temporary hold and a flip flop for storage. Give a practical example where this combination is useful.
\end{enumerate}

