\lessonlabel{8}
\section{Counters and Registers}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define a binary counter and describe its internal operation in detail;
  \item distinguish between different types of shift registers and explain how data moves through them;
  \item read, interpret and draw timing diagrams for both counters and registers, including clock edges, propagation delays and intermediate states;
  \item compare ripple‑carry and synchronous counters, and identify common design mistakes.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Binary counters}

A \term{binary counter} is a sequential circuit whose output represents an integer that increases by one for each active clock transition. The circuit is built from flip‑flops, each of which stores one binary digit (bit). The least‑significant bit (LSB) toggles on every clock pulse; each more significant bit toggles only when all lower‑order bits are at logic 1. This behaviour mimics the way a mechanical odometer rolls over.

\paragraph{Ripple‑carry counter.}
In a ripple‑carry counter each flip‑flop's toggle output (Q) feeds the clock input of the next higher flip‑flop. The name comes from the fact that a change in the LSB ``ripples'' through the chain. For a four‑bit ripple counter the toggle condition of flip‑flop $Q_i$ is the logical AND of all lower‑order outputs. Because the ripple travels sequentially, the propagation delay grows with the number of bits, which limits the maximum operating frequency.

\paragraph{Synchronous counter.}
A synchronous counter eliminates the ripple delay by driving every flip‑flop from the same clock source. Instead of cascading clocks, the toggle condition for each stage is generated combinationally from the current outputs. For a four‑bit synchronous counter the toggle inputs are:
\[
\begin{aligned}
T_0 &= 1 \\
T_1 &= Q_0 \\
T_2 &= Q_0 Q_1 \\
T_3 &= Q_0 Q_1 Q_2
\end{aligned}
\]
All flip‑flops receive the clock simultaneously, so the whole word changes in one clock period. The trade‑off is a larger combinational network and higher power consumption.

\paragraph{Common mistakes.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Forgetting to initialise the counter to a known state (usually 0000). Without a reset, the circuit may start in an undefined configuration.
  \item Using a rising‑edge clock for some flip‑flops and a falling‑edge clock for others, which destroys the intended counting sequence.
  \item Ignoring the possibility of a glitch when multiple toggle conditions change at the same time; proper gating or using edge‑triggered flip‑flops avoids this problem.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
Design a three‑bit synchronous binary counter using T flip‑flops. The toggle inputs are derived from the current outputs as follows:
\[
T_0 = 1,\qquad T_1 = Q_0,\qquad T_2 = Q_0 Q_1.
\]
Assume the counter is reset to 000. After each rising clock edge the state sequence is:
\[
000 \rightarrow 001 \rightarrow 010 \rightarrow 011 \rightarrow 100 \rightarrow 101 \rightarrow 110 \rightarrow 111 \rightarrow 000.
\]
Notice that the third flip‑flop ($Q_2$) changes only when both $Q_0$ and $Q_1$ are 1, exactly as the toggle equation predicts.
\end{example}

\subsection{Shift registers}

A \term{shift register} is a linear array of flip‑flops where the output of each stage is connected to the input of the next stage. On each clock pulse the stored bits move one position left or right, depending on the wiring. Shift registers are used for temporary storage, data serialization, and as building blocks for more complex sequential devices.

\paragraph{Serial‑in parallel‑out (SIPO).}
In a SIPO register a single serial data line feeds the first flip‑flop. After $n$ clock cycles, where $n$ is the number of stages, the complete word appears on the parallel outputs. This arrangement is useful when a high‑speed serial stream must be converted to a parallel bus for processing.

\paragraph{Parallel‑in serial‑out (PISO).}
A PISO register loads an entire word simultaneously through parallel inputs, typically using a load control signal. Once loaded, the register shifts the bits out one by one on successive clock edges. This configuration is common in communication interfaces where a parallel microcontroller must transmit data over a serial line.

\paragraph{Bidirectional and circular shift registers.}
Some designs allow shifting in either direction by providing separate left‑shift and right‑shift control signals. A circular (or rotate) shift register feeds the output of the last stage back into the first, creating a rotating pattern useful for timing generators and pseudo‑random sequence generators.

\paragraph{Common pitfalls.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Overlooking the need for a clear or preset input; without it the register may retain garbage data after power‑up.
  \item Forgetting to synchronise the serial input with the clock, which can cause metastability and incorrect bit capture.
  \item Assuming that all bits shift simultaneously; in a ripple‑type shift register the shift propagates stage by stage, adding delay.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A five‑bit PISO register is loaded with the binary word 10101 using a parallel load signal. The load is asserted for one clock cycle, after which the load signal is released and the register shifts right on each rising edge. The output sequence on the serial line $D_{out}$ is:
\[
1,\;0,\;1,\;0,\;1.
\]
During the shift, the parallel outputs change as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{Clock} & Q_4 Q_3 Q_2 Q_1 Q_0 \\ \hline
0 & 1\;0\;1\;0\;1 \\
1 & 0\;1\;0\;1\;0 \\
2 & 1\;0\;1\;0\;0 \\
3 & 0\;1\;0\;0\;0 \\
4 & 1\;0\;0\;0\;0 \\
\end{array}
\]
The example illustrates how the loaded word is serialised while the remaining bits shift toward the least‑significant position.
\end{example}

\subsection{Timing diagrams}

A \term{timing diagram} is a graphical representation that plots logical levels of signals against time. For counters and shift registers the diagram typically includes the clock waveform, any control signals (reset, load, direction), the serial data line (for shift registers), and the individual flip‑flop outputs. Timing diagrams are essential for verifying that a design meets functional specifications and for spotting race conditions.

\paragraph{Elements of a timing diagram.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Clock waveform}: drawn as a series of high and low intervals; rising or falling edges are marked where state changes are triggered.
  \item \textbf{Control signals}: such as RESET (active high or low) and LOAD; their asserted periods are shown as constant high or low levels.
  \item \textbf{Data waveforms}: for shift registers, the serial input $D_{in}$ and serial output $D_{out}$ are plotted alongside the parallel outputs $Q_i$.
  \item \textbf{State annotations}: often the binary value of the whole register is written above the group of $Q_i$ lines at each clock edge.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Reading a diagram.}
To determine the state after a given clock edge, locate the rising (or falling) edge on the clock line, then read the levels of each $Q_i$ immediately after that edge. Propagation delay appears as a slight lag between the clock edge and the change in $Q_i$; in a ripple‑carry counter this lag increases with bit position.

\paragraph{Common errors in diagrams.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Misaligning the clock edge with the output change, which suggests an unrealistic zero‑delay circuit.
  \item Omitting the reset pulse at the start; without it the initial state is ambiguous.
  \item Forgetting to indicate the direction of shift in a bidirectional register, leading to confusion about which output line carries the next bit.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
The timing diagram below shows a three‑bit synchronous counter with a rising‑edge clock, an active‑high reset, and outputs $Q_2$, $Q_1$, $Q_0$.

\begin{itemize}
  \item Clock: six rising edges.
  \item Reset: high for the first half‑cycle, then low.
  \item $Q_0$: 0 1 0 1 0 1.
  \item $Q_1$: 0 0 1 1 0 0.
  \item $Q_2$: 0 0 0 0 1 1.
\end{itemize}

At the third rising edge the outputs are $Q_2 Q_1 Q_0 = 010$, representing decimal two. The diagram confirms that all three flip‑flops change simultaneously, as expected for a synchronous design.
\end{example}

\subsection{Ring counters}

A \term{ring counter} is a special type of shift register where only a single `1' circulates among a chain of flip‑flops. The output of the last stage is fed back to the input of the first, creating a closed loop. Ring counters are useful for sequencing, LED chasers and generating evenly spaced pulses.

\paragraph{Operation.}
If the register contains four stages and is initialised to 1000, each clock pulse shifts the `1' one position to the right. The sequence of states is 1000, 0100, 0010, 0001, and then back to 1000. Because only one bit is high at any time, the circuit consumes less power than a full binary counter of the same size.

\paragraph{Design tip.}
A clear or preset input should be used to load the initial `1' pattern. Without proper initialisation the ring may start in an all‑zero state, which will never produce a moving `1'.

\begin{example}
Design a three‑stage ring counter that starts in state 100. After each rising clock edge the states are:
\[
100 \rightarrow 010 \rightarrow 001 \rightarrow 100.
\]
The feedback connection routes $Q_0$ back to the data input of the first flip‑flop. This simple pattern can drive three LEDs in a rotating fashion.
\end{example}

